1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,655
Sam noticed it while checking comments on a Tuesday: two replies, minutes apart,

2
00:00:04,655 --> 00:00:05,946
under the exact same ad.

3
00:00:05,946 --> 00:00:10,197
One reader wrote "finally, someone gets it." Another asked, genuinely confused,

4
00:00:10,197 --> 00:00:13,587
"wait, what's compression?" Same sock, same headline, same day.

5
00:00:13,587 --> 00:00:16,762
Only one of those two people was actually ready to hear it.

6
00:00:16,762 --> 00:00:18,538
That's not a copywriting problem.

7
00:00:18,538 --> 00:00:23,004
It's a mistake that looks like efficiency and is actually the opposite of it: write

8
00:00:23,004 --> 00:00:27,255
one strong angle, generate twenty variations of it, run all twenty at one broad

9
00:00:27,255 --> 00:00:27,739
audience.

10
00:00:27,739 --> 00:00:28,708
It feels thorough.

11
00:00:28,708 --> 00:00:33,174
It's actually one bet, repeated twenty times, because it ignores the single biggest

12
00:00:33,174 --> 00:00:37,586
thing that changes whether an ad works: someone's awareness level, meaning how far

13
00:00:37,586 --> 00:00:41,998
up the ladder from "never thought about this" to "already a customer" they already

14
00:00:41,998 --> 00:00:43,774
are before they ever see your ad.

15
00:00:43,774 --> 00:00:48,240
This is easy to miss because the twenty variants really do look different from each

16
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:48,563
other.

17
00:00:48,563 --> 00:00:51,469
Different colours, different hooks, different formats.

18
00:00:51,469 --> 00:00:55,666
What they share, underneath the surface variety, is that every one of them was

19
00:00:55,666 --> 00:01:00,025
written for the same imagined viewer, and that viewer doesn't actually exist as a

20
00:01:00,025 --> 00:01:01,693
single person in your audience.

21
00:01:02,243 --> 00:01:06,660
Here's the ladder, and Harlan's actual audience sits on every rung of it at once.

22
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:11,076
Someone who's never thought about their sore feet as a solvable problem is at the

23
00:01:11,076 --> 00:01:11,458
bottom.

24
00:01:11,458 --> 00:01:15,929
Someone who already searched "compression socks for a twelve hour shift" last week

25
00:01:15,929 --> 00:01:19,038
is much higher up, and already knows the category exists.

26
00:01:19,038 --> 00:01:23,454
Someone who added a pack to their cart and didn't check out is nearly at the top,

27
00:01:23,454 --> 00:01:27,653
and needs a completely different sentence than the person on the bottom rung.

28
00:01:27,653 --> 00:01:32,016
Read down the right-hand column and notice it isn't a ladder of "harder sell" to

29
00:01:32,016 --> 00:01:35,069
"easier sell." It's a ladder of different jobs entirely.

30
00:01:35,069 --> 00:01:37,032
The bottom rung is an education job.

31
00:01:37,032 --> 00:01:38,614
The top rung is a memory job.

32
00:01:38,614 --> 00:01:42,921
Treating both as "convince them to buy" is where a single generic angle quietly

33
00:01:42,921 --> 00:01:44,394
fails at both ends at once.

34
00:01:44,394 --> 00:01:48,374
The comment Sam found belongs on this table too, if you want to place it.

35
00:01:48,374 --> 00:01:52,900
"Finally, someone gets it" reads like solution-aware or higher: someone who already

36
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:56,063
knew the category and just needed Harlan's specific proof.

37
00:01:56,063 --> 00:02:00,643
"Wait, what's compression?" is unaware, full stop, and the exact reader that comment

38
00:02:00,643 --> 00:02:02,497
was answering never got asked for.

39
00:02:03,047 --> 00:02:06,938
Watch what happens when the level is wrong, because it's not a small miss.

40
00:02:06,938 --> 00:02:11,144
Introduce a brand to someone who already knows it, and you sound like you're not

41
00:02:11,144 --> 00:02:12,038
paying attention.

42
00:02:12,038 --> 00:02:16,402
Open with a hard sell to someone who's never heard of compression socks, and you've

43
00:02:16,402 --> 00:02:20,292
skipped the part where they'd need to believe the category matters at all.

44
00:02:20,292 --> 00:02:21,817
Both ads can be well written.

45
00:02:21,817 --> 00:02:25,602
Both will still underperform, because the mismatch isn't a copy problem.

46
00:02:25,602 --> 00:02:28,336
It's a targeting problem wearing copy as a disguise.

47
00:02:28,336 --> 00:02:32,700
This also explains a specific, confusing pattern you'll see in real accounts: an ad

48
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:36,959
that performs brilliantly on one audience and falls flat on another, with nothing

49
00:02:36,959 --> 00:02:38,431
about the ad itself changed.

50
00:02:38,431 --> 00:02:41,953
Usually the audience changed rungs, and the ad didn't move with it.

51
00:02:42,503 --> 00:02:46,737
Let's name the actual skill, because "know your audience" is true and useless as

52
00:02:46,737 --> 00:02:47,108
advice.

53
00:02:47,108 --> 00:02:51,500
Matching means writing the one sentence the audience needs next, not the one you're

54
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:52,135
proudest of.

55
00:02:52,135 --> 00:02:55,681
A cold audience needs the problem named before anything else lands.

56
00:02:55,681 --> 00:02:59,915
A warm audience needs their specific hesitation answered, directly, not a better

57
00:02:59,915 --> 00:03:02,455
version of the pitch they've already seen twice.

58
00:03:02,455 --> 00:03:04,836
Try this on something you've already shipped.

59
00:03:04,836 --> 00:03:08,329
Pick a real ad and guess, honestly, which rung it was written for.

60
00:03:08,329 --> 00:03:12,775
If the honest answer is "I'm not sure", that's usually because it wasn't written for

61
00:03:12,775 --> 00:03:13,516
a rung at all.

62
00:03:13,516 --> 00:03:17,061
It was written to sound good, and sounding good is a different job.

63
00:03:17,061 --> 00:03:20,819
This is also a useful way to read someone else's ad, not just your own.

64
00:03:20,819 --> 00:03:25,053
Scroll past a competitor's ad and ask the same question: what does this assume I

65
00:03:25,053 --> 00:03:25,899
already believe?

66
00:03:25,899 --> 00:03:28,122
Most ads answer that question by accident.

67
00:03:28,122 --> 00:03:29,921
Yours should answer it on purpose.

68
00:03:30,471 --> 00:03:35,011
I want to be honest about what this costs, because it's real work, and skipping it

69
00:03:35,011 --> 00:03:38,167
is exactly how most AI-generated batches end up mediocre.

70
00:03:38,167 --> 00:03:42,763
Matching properly means deciding, for every angle, which rung it's aimed at, before

71
00:03:42,763 --> 00:03:44,147
you write a word of copy.

72
00:03:44,147 --> 00:03:48,577
That's real thinking time, on top of the generation itself, and it's tempting to

73
00:03:48,577 --> 00:03:53,061
skip straight to "make me twenty ads." The batches that skip it are the ones that

74
00:03:53,061 --> 00:03:57,491
generate twenty technically-fine variants that all quietly say the same thing to

75
00:03:57,491 --> 00:03:57,989
everyone.

76
00:03:57,989 --> 00:04:01,477
The good news is that this cost is paid once, not twenty times.

77
00:04:01,477 --> 00:04:05,962
You decide the ladder for a product once, and every future batch for that product

78
00:04:05,962 --> 00:04:06,516
reuses it.

79
00:04:06,516 --> 00:04:10,945
It's expensive the first time and nearly free after that, which is a trade worth

80
00:04:10,945 --> 00:04:13,271
making early rather than avoiding forever.

81
00:04:13,271 --> 00:04:17,866
It's also the cost that would have caught Sam's comment problem before it ever ran.

82
00:04:17,866 --> 00:04:22,406
A ladder decided in advance means you're choosing, on purpose, which rungs a given

83
00:04:22,406 --> 00:04:27,057
ad is for, instead of finding out from a confused comment after the spend is already

84
00:04:27,057 --> 00:04:27,334
gone.

85
00:04:27,884 --> 00:04:32,271
Next lecture gives you the actual patterns for the top of each ad: the hooks that

86
00:04:32,271 --> 00:04:36,604
work at each rung, named clearly enough that you can brief a generator with them

87
00:04:36,604 --> 00:04:38,500
instead of hoping it guesses right.

88
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:41,966
Keep the ladder from this lecture next to you while you read it.

89
00:04:41,966 --> 00:04:46,516
The hook taxonomy is really this same ladder, made specific enough to actually write

90
00:04:46,516 --> 00:04:46,786
from.

91
00:04:46,786 --> 00:04:49,440
Before then, do the five-minute version yourself.

92
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:53,719
For each traffic source you actually run right now, cold interest, retargeting,

93
00:04:53,719 --> 00:04:57,565
search, write down one word for which rung you think it mostly reaches.

94
00:04:57,565 --> 00:05:02,060
That guess becomes the first column of your own version of the ladder, and it's the

95
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:05,147
piece most people skip on the way to building B01 in 2.6.
